WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.090
Narrator: Would you eat this avocado?

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:03.670
How about this one?

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.190
They're actually the same,

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.910
just one's been sitting
out for a few hours.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.570
And sure, one doesn't look as good,

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.120
but that doesn't mean you
should just throw it out.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.480
In 1989, the average
American ate about one pound

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.030
of avocados a year.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:25.030
By 2017, that amount
had increased sevenfold.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.040
But as much as we like to eat them,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.250
the struggle with
browning avocados is real.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:31.640
Some people might try to prevent it

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.930
by putting the avocado pit in the bowl,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.730
but it does not work that way.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.260
Just like with apples,
bananas, and potatoes,

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.750
the flesh of an avocado
browns when it's exposed

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.340
to oxygen in the air.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.800
It's a process called oxidation,

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.110
and it happens when the
oxygen reacts with compounds

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.410
called polyphenols with the help

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.550
of enzymes called polyphenol oxidase.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.550
This damages the tissue of the flesh,

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.690
in the process turning it brown.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.270
But the brown color is not
a sign that it's spoiling.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.580
You'd have to leave it out for a few days

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.980
before it spoiled from oxidation.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.340
That brown color is actually

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.160
from a nontoxic chemical called melanin.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.240
It shows up in everything from fruit

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.190
to the iris in your eyes.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.560
Needless to say, that
bowl of brown avocado

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.030
won't hurt you.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.630
However, the more
oxidation that takes place,

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.850
the more tissues it damages.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.350
For example, bruising
or chilling an avocado

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:28.010
for too long can lead to
significant oxidation,

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.060
which actually damages so much tissue

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.910
that it changes the texture
and flavor of the fruit

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.780
so that it's mushy and bitter.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.240
But a little browning
won't make a difference.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.420
To prove it, we did an experiment.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.860
We served green avocados and avocados

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.193
that were left to brown for a few hours.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.417
Gina Echevarria: "Okay,
tastes like a normal avocado,

00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:55.250
I think."

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.627
Abby Tang: "I don't like that one.

00:01:57.627 --> 00:01:58.469
I don't know why.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.633
I just like, it tastes a little bad."

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.310
Clancy Morgan: "Pretty
strong taste, I would say.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.840
Pretty strong avocado, earthy taste.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.440
But yeah, it tastes very ripe."

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.143
Echevarria: "Yeah,
pretty standard avocado."

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.410
Tang: "It tastes like a
regular avocado but rotten.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.615
Why did you feed me a rotten avocado?"

00:02:23.781 --> 00:02:24.614
Echevarria: "I feel like
that one actually tastes

00:02:24.614 --> 00:02:25.447
a little bit better."

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:27.423
Morgan: "Oh.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.650
Oof."

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.240
Tang: "This one doesn't taste
rotten, so that's good."

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.110
Morgan: "Okay, that is

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.250
much more ripe, super mushy."

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.016
Echevarria: "The other one
kind of just tasted like

00:02:43.017 --> 00:02:44.333
wet paper,

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.320
and this one actually
tasted like something."

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.670
Tang: "This one's fine, I like it enough.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:49.920
It's an avocado.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.440
They taste the same other
than one tasted older."

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.310
Cameraperson: "And that's the first one?"

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.948
Tang: "The first one tasted older.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.063
It was, like, sour.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.620
And the second one tasted
like a regular avocado."

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.150
Narrator: How fast your avocado browns

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.430
depends on the environment,
like how much oxygen

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.890
is in the air, what the temperature is,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.620
and how acidic it is.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.380
Warmer temperatures accelerate
the chemical reaction,

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:15.400
turning your avocado brown faster.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.440
But if you squirt some citrus on the top,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.420
the acidity prevents oxidation,

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.950
and you can keep it
looking fresher for longer.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.840
Either way, even though we couldn't agree

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.020
on which tasted better,
both were perfectly edible.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.970
So next time you have some old avocado,

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.510
don't just throw it out.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.880
And if the unsightly brown
ruins you're appetite,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.973
just give it a quick stir, and voila.

